window.docContents[95].resolve({"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Finite-Time-Behavior--Finite-Time-Growth___-Lifespan___-and-Similarity--Solutions___-Growth___-and-Continuation":{"contents":"A formula that grows quickly is only a candidate. A finite-time singularity\nstatement exhibits a solution on times before some finite horizon and proves that\na specified norm or observable becomes unbounded as that horizon approaches. We\nformalize the pieces separately: the solution concept, the maximal-time or\nnorm-divergence predicate, the mechanism forcing the growth, and its application\nto the original PDE.\n\nWith the pieces kept apart, different arguments can be compared. Explicit\nsolutions, Riccati comparison, weighted moments, virial identities, and\nself-similar profiles all appear, and each proves a different kind of\nconclusion.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Finite-Time Behavior\u0009Finite-Time Growth, Lifespan, and Similarity","header":"6.7.1.1. Solutions, Growth, and Continuation","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Finite-Time-Behavior--Finite-Time-Growth___-Lifespan___-and-Similarity--Solutions___-Growth___-and-Continuation"}});